Print Media

What is it
- Newspapers
- Digital editions

Impartiality

- Not bound by impartiality
- Right-wing tabloids (The Daily Mail and The Daily Express) have often been ruthless in their attacks on Labour party in charged headlines and opinion pieces. 2016 - kill vampire Jezza days after the brutal murder of Jo Cox
    - The Daily Telegraph is firmly Conservative
    - The Times and Sunday Times have a reputation for greater impartiality
    - The Guardian’s progressive bias makes it a natural home for Liberal Democrats
    - Only Mirror Group newspapers have consistently supported the Labour Party
- The reputation that newspapers have for opinion-forming has made them attractive to business tycoons such as Robert Maxwell, Conrad Black and Rupert Murdoch since it enabled them to wield influence in politics
- Stanley Baldwin in 1931 when he charged that the Daily Mail and the Daily Express ‘are engines of propaganda for the constantly changing policies, desires, persons, vices. personal likes and dislikes of he two men (Lord Rothermere and Lord Beaverbrook). What are their methods? Their methods are direct falsehoods, misrepresentation, half-truths, the alteration of the speaker’s meaning publishing a sentence apart from the context… What the proprietorship of these papers is aiming at is power, and power without responsibility …’
- Currently six unaccountable media tycoons with a pronounced right-wing agenda (Lord Rothermere, Rupert Murdoch, Richard Desmond, the Barclay Brothers and Evgeny Lebedev) share vast influence over the sorts of stories UK citizens encounter when they read newspaper coverage either in print or online

Impact

- Rupert Murdoch trying to persuade John Major to change Conservative party policy on Europe
