What is it -
- Facebook, Twitter, Instagram, TikTok, Snapchat
- Facebook - 51% BBC, Twitter - 61% any public individual
- 47% of all UK adults use social media for news
- 71% of 16-24 year olds access news through social media

Impartiality -
- Irregulated
- If users have a politically partisan search history they get bombarded with results

Guardian video -
- In 2015, the Conservatives spent £1.2m on Facebook electioneering
- In 2015, Labour spent just £160,000
- In 2017, Labour said they will match the Tories and spend £1m
- Cambridge Analytica, analysing digital footprints, psychographic messaging
- Same policy phrased in different ways to different people e.g. gun policy → for people who are anxious you target those feelings, for people who want tradition do that
- Information Commissioner is investigating online campaigning,
- Online is still the wild west

Impact on elections -

For:

- Facebook advertising - At the start of December 2019, the Facebook Ad Library showed the Conservatives had 2500 live paid-for adverts, while Labour only had 250, and the Conservatives went on to seal a huge victory over Labour
- Although in 2022 the BBC was still the dominant news channel on social media, on TIkTok, which is especially popular among young people, the right-wing GB News was increasingly dominant.
- This can abe to the advantage of politicians and spread important news. During the 2019 general election, ‘Get Brexit Done’ was a simple and arresting message that spread quickly on social media, to the advantage of the Conservative party.

Against:

- At the same time, the Liberal Democrats had 3000 paid ads on Facebook, more then the Conservative Party, yet it lost seats
- In the 2017 general election, Labour had double the number of Conservative twitter users. Some critics suggest that this helped Labour to increase its vote share by 9.6%

